  <section class="faq" id="faq">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Questions drivers ask</h2>
      <div class="faq-list">

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What about my insurance?</summary>
          <div class="body">
            <p>
              Seat Sherpa is cost-sharing, not commercial driving — same legal category as splitting gas with friends. Your personal auto insurance usually covers it the same way it would cover any personal trip, because our <a href="#" onclick="document.querySelectorAll('details.faq-item').forEach(d=>{if(d.querySelector('summary').textContent.includes('max price per seat')){d.setAttribute('open','');d.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'center'});}});return false;" style="color:#00D4C8;">per-seat price cap</a> keeps you in true cost-sharing territory and away from anything an insurer would call "commercial."
            </p>
            <p>
              The one wrinkle: many personal auto policies contain a "livery exclusion" — language that excludes coverage when the vehicle is used "for hire." A reasonable insurer reading it correctly should say "cost-sharing isn't for-hire, you're covered." An aggressive claims adjuster looking for a reason to deny might point to the app-based payment and refuse to pay. You can't predict which interpretation a specific adjuster will pick when it matters.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Before your first trip, take 5 minutes to do one of these:</strong>
            </p>
            <ol>
              <li>Call your insurance carrier, disclose that you'll be using Seat Sherpa to share trip costs, and get a quick answer on whether your policy covers it. For most carriers and most policies, the answer will be yes.</li>
              <li>If you want extra peace of mind, add a <strong>ride-sharing endorsement</strong> (sometimes called a "rideshare rider") to your policy. Most CA/NV carriers — Mercury, Allstate, Progressive, State Farm, USAA — offer this for $15–$30/month. It explicitly removes any ambiguity in the livery exclusion.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
              You're driving on your own insurance — Seat Sherpa doesn't currently provide platform liability coverage. Knowing your coverage situation before you drive is the single most important thing you can do.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How does payment work?</summary>
          <div class="body">
            Riders pay through the app via Stripe when they book. The money is held securely until you enter the rider's 4-digit pickup code at the curb — that confirms the trip started and releases your share (95% of each seat) to your bank account through your connected Stripe account, paid out about 2 days later. The 5% we keep covers credit card processing and the platform's operating costs. Stripe handles all card details; we never see your card or your rider's.
          </div>
        </details>

        <!-- Drive Rewards FAQ removed 2026-07-31 with the banner — same
             reason: 0122/0123 unrun, so neither the credit nor the 90-day
             expiry it described actually exists yet. -->
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How does ride credit work? Does it expire?</summary>
          <div class="body">
            Ride credit (from referrals or promo codes) applies automatically to your next
            booking — you'll see it deducted on the payment screen. It can't be transferred or
            cashed out, and <strong>all credit expires 180 days after the day it's granted</strong>.
            Oldest credit is always used first, so whatever you spend counts against the credit
            closest to expiring.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why is there a max price per seat?</summary>
          <div class="body">
            <p>
              Seat Sherpa is a <strong>cost-sharing carpool platform</strong>, not a commercial transport service like Uber or Lyft. The legal difference matters: cost-sharing carpools don't need to be licensed as Transportation Network Companies (TNCs), which keeps everyone's life simpler — no commercial insurance requirements, no special permits, no $1M policies, lower fees.
            </p>
            <p>
              For that to hold up, drivers <strong>can't profit</strong> from a trip — they can only recover what the trip actually costs to drive. The IRS publishes a standard mileage rate ($0.76/mile as of July 1, 2026) that's universally accepted as a proxy for the real cost of operating a personal vehicle: gas, wear, depreciation, insurance. We use that as our ceiling:
            </p>
            <p style="background:#1a1a1d;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);border-radius:8px;padding:14px 18px;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace;font-size:13px;color:#ccc;">
              max per-seat price = (trip distance × $0.76) ÷ seats offered (at least 4)
            </p>
            <p>
              Examples (4 passenger seats):
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>LA → San Diego (~120 mi) → max $22/seat</li>
              <li>LA → Vegas (~270 mi) → max $51/seat (IRS cap, still under the $55 ceiling)</li>
              <li>Bay → LA (~380 mi) → would calc to $72, capped at $55 (our hard ceiling)</li>
              <li>Bay → Las Vegas (~570 mi) → would calc to $108, capped at $55</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              On longer routes the IRS-rate cap is generous enough that our $55 brand ceiling takes over. On shorter routes the IRS cap binds. Either way, you can't price yourself into "commercial driver" territory by accident — when you post a ride the app shows the exact max for your route (trip distance × $0.76 ÷ seats offered, counted as at least four) and won't let you set a seat price above it.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>This is also why a rideshare endorsement isn't strictly required</strong> (see the insurance FAQ above): you're staying squarely in cost-sharing territory, which is what your personal auto insurance is designed to cover. The endorsement is a belt-and-suspenders option if you want extra peace of mind, not a legal necessity.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What if a rider doesn't show up?</summary>
          <div class="body">
            If they no-show, you don't enter their pickup code — and the booking stays in limbo until you cancel it with a reason. Per our cancellation policy, they may forfeit some or all of their fare depending on how close to departure it was. You drive your trip with the other riders (if any) and the no-show pays the cancellation fee. You can also block a no-show rider so you don't see them on future bookings.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What if I have to cancel?</summary>
          <div class="body">
            Things happen. If you cancel a confirmed booking, riders get a 100% refund automatically. Cancelling once or twice is fine; doing it routinely will affect your driver rating and your ride visibility in the marketplace. We expect drivers to honor the commitments they post.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How much does this actually offset my driving costs?</summary>
          <div class="body">
            Quite a bit, on the right trips. On an LA→Vegas run in a 5-seat car at $35/seat, you'd receive $133 after our 5% fee. A round-trip fill-up for that route runs $90–$120, so rider contributions cover all your fuel — and a 7-seat van on Bay→LA saves up to $228, more than enough for gas, wear, and a meal at the destination. The IRS standard mileage rate ($0.76/mile, which accounts for gas + wear + depreciation + insurance) is the ceiling we enforce: you can't price above that. The result is cost-sharing, not commercial driving.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Do I get the carpool lane and toll discounts?</summary>
          <div class="body">
            Often, yes — a real carpool has real perks. With two or more people in the car you can use HOV/carpool lanes to skip traffic, and with a FasTrak Flex tag set to your headcount, carpools get discounted (sometimes free) tolls in Bay Area express lanes and on the bridges. Heads up so you don't get dinged: most carpool lanes need 2+ occupants, but some — including the San Francisco–Oakland Bay Bridge — need 3+, and the toll discounts only apply during posted carpool hours. Follow the signs for the lane you're in.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How do you screen riders?</summary>
          <div class="body">
            Every rider verifies their phone via SMS at signup. They get a rating after every ride that other drivers see. You can also block riders you don't want to see again — they'll never appear on your bookings or in your inbox. Two-way ratings build trust over time so the platform self-cleans.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What's the 4-digit pickup code thing?</summary>
          <div class="body">
            It's how we confirm a trip actually happened. Each rider sees a unique 4-digit code in their app. At pickup, you ask them for it and enter it in your app — that marks the trip as started and unlocks the "Mark complete" button. Without the code, the trip doesn't count and you don't get paid. It's a low-friction safety net against fraud on both sides (drivers claiming trips that didn't happen, riders ghosting after paying).
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What kind of car do I need?</summary>
          <div class="body">
            Any safe, registered, insured passenger vehicle works — even a 2-seat coupe. You set the number of passenger seats you can offer when you post a ride (1 to 7), so the more seats you can share, the more of your trip costs get covered. Sedans, SUVs, vans, hybrids, EVs, pickups, coupes — all welcome. We verify your registration and license at signup. No specific make, model, or year requirements.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Where can I drive?</summary>
          <div class="body">
            Anywhere within California and Nevada, for now. The most popular routes are SF↔LA, Bay↔Vegas, LA↔Vegas, LA↔San Diego, and LA↔Palm Springs, but you can post any route — including small towns, college campuses, and so on. We'll expand to Arizona and Oregon once CA/NV is humming.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How do taxes work?</summary>
          <div class="body">
            Driving income is taxable. Stripe will issue you a 1099 if you earn $600+ in a calendar year. You're treated as an independent contractor — track your mileage, gas receipts, and a portion of vehicle maintenance as deductible business expenses. We're not tax advisors; consult one if your driving volume is significant.
          </div>
        </details>

      </div>
    </div>
